This is a web application based on Spot.  It offers some
tools to rewrite/study/compare/translate LTL formula.

It's made of
- a server-side API written in Python using Spot's Python
  binding,
- a client-side web application written using ReactJS,
  and calling that API.


Development
-----------

To start developping, first install Spot (see http://spot.lre.epita.fr/install.html)
with its Python bindings.  Then launch an API server on localhost:5000 with

  % server/spotapi.py

finally launch a web client with

  % cd client
  % npm run start

The client runs with Vite on http://localhost:3000/app/ and proxies /api
requests to the Flask server on localhost:5000.  Now any save to spotapi.py
or files under client/src/ will cause the components to reload automatically.


Client-only build
-----------------

The application can also be built so that it never contacts a server:
Spot and GraphViz are then compiled to WebAssembly and run in the
browser, so the formulas typed in the page do not leave the computer.
The resulting directory can be served by any static web server.

The API is then answered by wasm/spotapi.cc, a C++ port of
server/spotapi.py, running in a Web Worker (client/src/api/local/).
Changes to spotapi.py should be mirrored there.

To build it, install Emscripten (https://emscripten.org/), and build
with it a version of Spot that can be compiled to WebAssembly (its NEWS
says so: older versions miss two #include <algorithm>):

  % emconfigure ./configure --host=wasm32-unknown-emscripten \
      --disable-shared --disable-python --prefix=$HOME/spot-wasm \
      CFLAGS='-O2 -fwasm-exceptions' CXXFLAGS='-O2 -fwasm-exceptions' \
      LDFLAGS=-fwasm-exceptions --disable-debug \
      gl_cv_func_sleep_works=yes
  % for d in cadical buddy lib ltdl spot; do emmake make -C $d; done
  % emmake make -C buddy install
  % emmake make -C spot install

(or download the spot-wasm.tar.gz artifact of the "emscripten" job of
Spot's pipeline), and then

  % make -C wasm SPOT_PREFIX=$HOME/spot-wasm
  % cd client
  % npm run test               # now also tests the WebAssembly module
  % VITE_SPOT_BACKEND=wasm npm run build

The application is then in client/build/, and expects to be served
at /app/ (e.g., at http://localhost:8080/app/).  To serve it from
another path, give that path to the build, as in

  % VITE_SPOT_BACKEND=wasm npm run build -- --base=/

for the root of a web server.  The deep links of the application
(e.g., /app/translate?f=GFa) need the server to answer index.html for
paths it does not know.  "VITE_SPOT_BACKEND=wasm npm run start" runs
the application in development mode, without the Flask server.


Using remotely or locally
-------------------------

This application is live at https://spot.lre.epita.fr/app/

However this public server may be subject to occasional down time.  If
you absolutely need the application for critical work (e.g, student
exam) you should run it locally on your own computer.  This can be
done from git using the development instruction above, or more simply
by downloading a copy of the docker container that runs the Spot web
site:

   sudo docker pull gitlab-registry.lre.epita.fr/spot/spot-web
   sudo docker run --rm=true -p 8080:80 gitlab-registry.lre.epita.fr/spot/spot-web

The first command is optionnal and can be used to update an old local
copy of the container.  The second command will run the container and
map its internal 80 port to 8080 on the local host, so you can point
your browser to localhost:8080/app/ to access the application.


